---
/**
 * /guides/consumables - the "which potion when" guide.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory classic potion guide', 'best potions
 * maplestory beginner', 'maplestory classic elixir vs power elixir',
 * 'maplestory potion efficiency'. Beginners routinely overspend on
 * potions because they assume "bigger number = better" - reality is
 * mesos-per-HP degrades sharply above White Potion tier, so Red
 * Potion spam remains competitive well into Lv 40.
 *
 * All potion HP/MP values and NPC prices below come directly from
 * items.json (spec.hp / spec.mp / npcSellPrice fields). Numbers are
 * v83-era MSCW; verify against a shop in-game if a value looks off.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ItemRewardStrip from "../../components/ItemRewardStrip.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Consumables Guide - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="HP potions, MP potions, elixirs, buff foods, and status cures for MapleStory Classic. Real mesos-per-HP math from the datamine, when to switch tiers, and the potion overspending trap that eats new-player wallets."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="ellinia"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">Beginner Reference</span>
			<h1>Consumables Guide</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Which potion at which level, why bigger-number-potions are usually
				worse per meso, and the elixir tier that quietly saves 60% of your
				potion budget past Lv 50. All HP/MP/price numbers below are pulled
				straight from the CoT 2 datamine.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<h2 id="tldr">The 10-second TL;DR</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Lv 1-15:</strong> Apples + Red Potions. Don't buy anything fancier.</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 15-30:</strong> Red Potions still, plus Blue Potions once you have skills that cost MP.</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 30-50:</strong> Orange Potions for HP, Pure Water for MP. Bank Elixirs from drops.</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 50-70:</strong> White Potions for HP, Sunrise/Sunset Dew for MP, Power Elixir as emergency.</li>
			<li><strong>Lv 70+:</strong> Power Elixirs as bosses, otherwise mesos-per-heal cliff kicks in and White + Sunset Dew is still fine.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Full math + why, below.
		</p>

		<h2 id="hp-progression">HP potion progression, with actual mesos-per-HP</h2>
		<p>
			Every HP potion has a fixed heal value and a fixed NPC buy price
			(NPCs sell at 1x, buy back at ~40%). The efficiency metric that
			matters is <strong>mesos-per-HP-restored</strong>. Higher-tier
			potions <em>heal more per press</em>, but the meso cost climbs
			faster than the heal amount does. That's why an Lv 60 Warrior who
			mainlines White Potions burns 3x more meso than one who alternates
			Orange Potions with Sunrise Dew.
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="The HP potion ladder"
			lede="Sorted low to high. Note that Apple (Lv 1-8) and Red Potion (Lv 8+) are tied for best mesos-per-HP in the whole game at 20 HP/meso. It gets worse from there."
			itemIds={[2010001, 2000000, 2000001, 2000002]}
			notes={{
				2010001: "40 HP / 2 mesos (20 HP/meso)",
				2000000: "100 HP / 5 mesos (20 HP/meso)",
				2000001: "250 HP / 15 mesos (16.7 HP/meso)",
				2000002: "500 HP / 35 mesos (14.3 HP/meso)",
			}}
		/>

		<h3>Why Red Potion never really dies</h3>
		<p>
			Red Potion stays at the exact same efficiency as the very first
			Apple you ever bought - 20 HP per meso. Orange loses 17% efficiency.
			White loses 29%. If you die when a big pot press was going to save
			you, you spent 1000+ mesos on a death. If you spam Red Potions
			twice as often, you're spending the same 1000 mesos across two
			presses and get the same net HP with a much fatter safety margin.
		</p>
		<p>
			The <em>only</em> reason to upgrade is when your max HP outgrows the
			pot value - a Lv 40 Warrior with 3000 max HP is losing efficiency
			on Red Potions because they overheal-cap. But most non-Warriors
			don't cross that threshold until Lv 45+.
		</p>

		<h2 id="mp-progression">MP potion progression</h2>
		<p>
			MP potions don't have an Apple-tier freebie, so the ladder starts
			at Green Apple (30 MP for 3 mesos = 10 MP/meso) and gradually
			erodes from there. Mages and MP-hungry classes will get the most
			benefit from the tier changes because they're pressing pot buttons
			literally between every mob.
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="The MP potion ladder"
			lede="Blue Potion stays the workhorse until Lv 30ish for most classes. Pure Water is the mid-game Mage's best friend at 800 MP a press. Sunset Dew is the highest per-press mid-game MP restore."
			itemIds={[2000003, 2020009, 2022000, 2020016, 2020017]}
			notes={{
				2000003: "200 MP / 22 mesos (9.1 MP/meso)",
				2020009: "450 MP / 54 mesos (8.3 MP/meso, Orange Juice)",
				2022000: "800 MP / 112 mesos (7.1 MP/meso)",
				2020016: "2000 MP / 300 mesos (6.7 MP/meso, Sunrise Dew)",
				2020017: "2500 MP / 375 mesos (6.7 MP/meso, Sunset Dew)",
			}}
		/>

		<h2 id="elixir-tier">The elixir tier: percent-based full heals</h2>
		<p>
			Elixirs are a completely different mechanic than the mesos-per-HP
			potions above - they restore a <strong>percentage of your
			maximum</strong>, not a fixed number. That makes them
			progressively better as your max HP/MP scale up, and turns them
			into full-heal buttons at high levels.
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="The elixir stack"
			lede="Don't buy these from NPCs at the listed prices - farm them from mob drops and hoard for boss fights. Power Elixir is basically 'oh no' insurance in a boss run."
			itemIds={[2000004, 2000006, 2000005]}
			notes={{
				2000004: "35% HP + 35% MP (Elixir)",
				2000006: "50% MP (Mana Elixir)",
				2000005: "100% HP + 100% MP (Power Elixir)",
			}}
		/>

		<p>
			<strong>The Power Elixir rule:</strong> never NPC-buy them. NPC
			price is 2500 mesos, but they routinely drop from mid-tier mobs
			(Zombie Lupins, Selkies, various Sleepywood mobs). Bank every drop
			for boss runs where 1500 mesos for a full heal + full MP could be
			the difference between clear and wipe.
		</p>

		<h2 id="dual-recovery">Dual HP/MP items: the hidden meta</h2>
		<p>
			A handful of items restore both HP and MP simultaneously. These are
			mathematically better than pressing an HP potion AND an MP potion
			separately, provided you actually need both. The two headliners:
		</p>

		<ItemRewardStrip
			title="Dual HP/MP restores"
			lede="Cake is the newbie hidden gem (100 HP + 100 MP for 15 mesos). Mrs. Ming Ming's Stew and Andre's Seafood Soup are mid-game dual pots that beat pressing two separate potions on cost per action."
			itemIds={[2020002, 2020018, 2020019]}
			notes={{
				2020002: "100 HP + 100 MP / 15 mesos (Cake)",
				2020018: "300 HP + 300 MP / 48 mesos (Stew)",
				2020019: "300 HP + 300 MP / 48 mesos (Seafood Soup)",
			}}
		/>

		<p>
			Cake in particular is <em>strictly better</em> than Blue Potion
			from Lv 8-25: you get 100 MP AND 100 HP for 15 mesos, versus 200 MP
			alone for 22 mesos. If you're taking any damage while grinding,
			cake wins on every press.
		</p>

		<h2 id="buff-foods">Buff foods: temporary stat sticks</h2>
		<p>
			Some consumables aren't recoveries at all - they grant a temporary
			buff. The classic example is Warrior Potion, which gives +10 WATK
			for 10 minutes. That's a small permanent damage upgrade during a
			farming run, and stacks with your class buffs.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Warrior Potion (id 2002000):</strong> +10 WATK for 10
				minutes. Cheap enough to run continuously during long grinding
				sessions. Doesn't stack with other WATK potions - refresh, don't
				overlap.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Magician / Bowman / Thief Potions:</strong> equivalent
				buffs for other classes if they exist in the current build.
				Check <a href="/items">the items catalog</a> and search
				"Potion" to see all class buffs available in the datamine.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Selective / All-cure potions:</strong> cure a status
				debuff (curse, seal, weakness, poison). Bank a stack of 20 in
				every character's inventory. Some Sleepywood mobs and
				mini-bosses inflict statuses that will otherwise force you to
				sit through them for minutes.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="pet-auto-pot">Pet auto-pot: the quality-of-life spend</h2>
		<p>
			If you have a pet (or plan to buy one), configure it to auto-feed
			HP/MP potions when either drops below a threshold. Setup takes 60
			seconds and pays for itself the first time you accidentally
			tab-away during a Ludibrium PQ. Two rules of thumb:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>HP threshold: 60%.</strong> Lower than 40% and you'll
				die to a big hit before the pot fires. Higher than 70% and you
				waste potions on chip damage that would've regenerated with
				MapleStory's HP recovery skill.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>MP threshold: 40%.</strong> Most skills cost 10-25 MP
				per cast; you'll never come close to zero if the pot fires at
				40%. Set it lower for tighter budgets.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Feed the pet frequently.</strong> Pet closeness drops
				when hunger hits zero, and unfed pets stop auto-potting. Buy
				pet food in bulk any time you visit town.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="where-to-buy">Where to actually buy potions</h2>
		<p>
			Town potion NPCs charge MSRP. Better sources:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Kerning City potion shop</strong> stocks the widest
				early-game range at standard prices. Fine default.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Perion potion shop</strong> is the Warrior potion
				stopover - stock Warrior Potion here before every long grind.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Auction House</strong> often has bulk 100-stacks of
				Red/Orange Potions from players clearing storage, at 10-30%
				below NPC price. Worth a scan before every town restock.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quest turn-ins</strong> - some quests reward stacks of
				potions directly. Any time a quest reward looks like "10 White
				Potions", that's 350 mesos of instant value.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="the-overspending-trap">The overspending trap in one paragraph</h2>
		<p>
			The single biggest new-player wallet leak: buying White Potions at
			Lv 20 because they heal a bigger number. At Lv 20 you have ~800
			max HP; a White Potion overheals for 500 - you're paying 35 mesos
			for the same effective heal as a 5-meso Red Potion, five times the
			cost. Multiply by 200 pots per grind session and you've spent 6000
			mesos on nothing. The rule: <strong>keep your potion heal value
			at or below your max HP such that at least 60% of the heal actually
			restores HP.</strong> Under that rule, most players stay on Red
			Potions until Lv 35, Orange until Lv 55, and only touch White
			after that.
		</p>

		<p class="closing">
			Related reading: <a href="/guides/items-to-keep">Items You Should
			Never Sell</a> covers the quest-turn-in ETC drops you want to bank
			(most give potions as rewards), and <a href="/guides/party-exp">
			Party EXP &amp; Grinding</a> covers how party leveling changes your
			potion budget (spoiler: party attackers spend way less on pots than
			solo).
		</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 72ch;
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.62;
		color: var(--text-body);
	}
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose h3 {
		margin-top: 1.4rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.15rem;
	}
	.prose p,
	.prose ul,
	.prose ol {
		margin-top: 0.7rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 0.09em;
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
	}
	.prose strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}
	.prose em {
		color: var(--text-body);
	}
	.prose .closing {
		margin-top: 2.2rem;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		font-style: italic;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}
</style>
